#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 720.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106941} :activity/id #uuid "894700da-1228-5454-9314-6d3f6401726e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049653 :topic/id #uuid "0875855d-14c4-5fc3-84ce-551f108cb97d" :topic/math-academy-id 1285} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106941 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107174 :tutorial/content "The **scalar projection** of a vector $\\mathbf{a}$ onto another vector $\\mathbf{b}$ is the distance that the vector $\\mathbf{a}$ extends in the direction of $\\mathbf{b}.$\n\nFor example, consider two vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ shown below. Suppose we know that $|\\,\\mathbf{a}\\,|=2$ and that the angle between the vectors is $\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}.$\n\n![](images/a2/a28278cf12885ab7f845407ac2fa7f4ffa00dbe30849a3f8ff41f02e134ed3a0.png)\n\nTo illustrate the projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ along $\\mathbf{b},$ consider the point $P$ on the line that contains $\\mathbf{b}$ with $AP \\perp \\mathbf{b},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/fd/fd37bdb2a97b4125fb360990d3c4a36b48bcb50433de033a154799db00e474dd.png)\n\nThe length $OP$ is called the **scalar projection** of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}$ (or the **component** of $\\mathbf{a}$ along $\\mathbf{b}$). It is denoted as $\\text{comp}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\,\\mathbf{a},$ and we can compute it using cosine in $\\triangle OPA$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =OP \\\\  & =|\\,a\\,|\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis formula is the general definition of the scalar projection:\n\n$$\n\\text{comp}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\,\\mathbf{a}= |\\,\\mathbf{a}\\,| \\cos\\theta\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "0b270bce-4ef9-5021-bff5-83fdd5dd81e3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9666 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "ead51520-a33b-57db-b2e2-17600925edc0" :step/math-academy-id 517262 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106942}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106942 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107177 :tutorial/content "The scalar projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}$ can be negative. This happens when the vector $\\mathbf{a}$ extends in the direction opposite to that of $\\mathbf{b}.$\n\n![](images/c1/c19648ea2df1935e89e10df2f7f5ab33ad537a5fdb52bb97c3555c9a0365701e.png)\n\nFor example, consider two vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b},$ where it is known that \n\n$$\n|\\,\\mathbf{a}\\,|=2, \\qquad \\theta = 135^\\circ.\n$$\n\nNotice that the angle $\\theta$ between the vectors is obtuse. \n\nComputing the scalar projection of $\\mathbf a$ onto $\\mathbf b$ using the formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =|\\,a\\,|\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}({135}^{∘}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, if $90^\\circ < \\theta \\leq 180^\\circ,$ then $\\cos\\theta <0$ and the scalar projection will be negative." :tutorial/id #uuid "1a12d95f-dd94-5328-b375-defd27770173" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9672 :tutorial/title "Negative Scalar Projections"} :step/id #uuid "8c96264f-2d5e-5d28-9eee-2a426710bf83" :step/math-academy-id 517263 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106943}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106943 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105916 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105488 :question/id #uuid "1a24b403-754c-5424-8182-8cad3487deed" :question/math-academy-id "e-15606"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e598ad1e-cee5-5d5f-b2a9-c067e4625cf6" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048410} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048882}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106110} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106478} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237842} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237843} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262619} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292825} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299701} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299702} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299703} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299704} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299705} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338958} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338959} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379335} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379360} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379931} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379938}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Scalar Projection Using Angles and Magnitudes"} :step/id #uuid "2d5a659f-e8f6-5945-9dc7-2e5ec29379f3" :step/math-academy-id 517264 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106944}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106944 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107175 :tutorial/content "We have been using the following formula for the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\text{comp}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\,\\mathbf{a}= |\\,\\mathbf{a}\\,| \\cos\\theta\n$$\n\nHowever, sometimes we are not given the angle between two vectors. In such cases, we can solve for $\\cos \\theta$ in terms of the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & =|\\,a\\,|\\,|\\,b\\,|\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}\\theta  & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,a\\,|\\,|\\,b\\,|}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our original formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =|\\,a\\,|\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\  & =|\\,a\\,|\\cdot \\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,a\\,|\\,|\\,b\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,b\\,|}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, another general formula for the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,b\\,|}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f213d39a-e24c-5b66-905d-b756335a134e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9667 :tutorial/title "Deriving a General Formula"} :step/id #uuid "ad2f5288-2fb4-5487-aa34-f13e8f7a3796" :step/math-academy-id 517267 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106945}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106945 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105917 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105489 :question/id #uuid "da8a1521-86cc-5747-88a7-e2ec56010c11" :question/math-academy-id "e-15607"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "91691448-c59a-56dc-a775-d4e9c3297036" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050336}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106038} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106098} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237844} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237845} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237846} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237847} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262620} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287850} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287928} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287929} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287930} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287931} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287932} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287933} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292826} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292827} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292828} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299706} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299707} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299708} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299709} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299710} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379363} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379366}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Scalar Projection Using Components"} :step/id #uuid "60e398f4-b9c4-5e66-a233-ddc18f32275e" :step/math-academy-id 517268 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106946}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106946 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107176 :tutorial/content "Notice that if two vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are perpendicular, then the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}$ (or $\\mathbf{b}$ onto $\\mathbf{a}$) is equal to $0.$\n\nFor example, consider the following illustration. Notice that, since $\\mathbf{a} \\perp \\mathbf{b},$ the vector $\\mathbf{a}$ doesn't extend in the direction of $\\mathbf{b}$ at all. So, the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}$ is $0.$\n\n![](images/ef/efb79d8645a5df54d76c0199335aad61b258e400a98dc485ccd8b3abc8e0ba8d.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following fact:\n\n*If $\\mathbf{a}$ is a nonzero vector, its scalar projection onto another nonzero vector $\\mathbf{b}$ equals $0$ if and only if the vectors are perpendicular.*" :tutorial/id #uuid "6b0bca52-c737-5b73-a389-368042379fd3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9671 :tutorial/title "Projecting Perpendicular Vectors"} :step/id #uuid "894f7db7-c33c-511f-b992-4c84d889d0d4" :step/math-academy-id 517271 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106947}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106947 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105918 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105490 :question/id #uuid "4693b28f-df75-574e-8e49-bb89e162b107" :question/math-academy-id "e-15608"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a51e317c-f6b3-5704-afb4-bdfc79f1176f" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106213} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106214} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290487} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290488} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290489} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290490} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290491} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290492} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290493} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292829} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292830} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299726} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299731} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299732} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299736} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299737} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299738} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299739} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299740} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379342} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379369}] :knowledge-point/title "Projecting a Vector Onto a Perpendicular Vector"} :step/id #uuid "ef2bba94-8ee2-5969-aca7-a6f1fd8a43b3" :step/math-academy-id 517272}] :activity/title "Calculating a Scalar Projection" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105962}]}
